{"name":"Stone Bridge","hex":"#52706c","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"stone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard earthen substance that can form rocks; especially, such substance when regarded as a building material.","A piece of such material: a block of stone, rock or a pebble."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Constructed of stone.","Having the appearance of stone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stone"},{"term":"bridge","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A construction or natural feature that spans a divide.\n A construction spanning a waterway, ravine, or valley from a height, allowing for the passage of vehicles, pedestrians, trains, etc.","A construction spanning a waterway, ravine, or valley from a height, allowing for the passage of vehicles, pedestrians, trains, etc."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To be or make a bridge over something.","To span as if with a bridge."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bridge"}],"entries":[{"title":"Stone Bridge (Adana)","blocks":[{"text":"Taşköprü, historically known as Ponte Sarus, is a Roman bridge spanning the Seyhan River in Adana that was probably built in the first half of the second century AD. The bridge was a key link in ancient trade routes from the Mediterranean Sea to Anatolia and Persia. Until its closure in 2007, it was one of the oldest bridges in the world open to motorized vehicles. Since 2007 it has only carried foot traffic, and now hosts social and cultural events."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Ta%C5%9Fk%C3%B6pr%C3%BC%27n%C3%BCn_Panoramik_Foto%C4%9Fraf%C4%B1.jpg/330px-Ta%C5%9Fk%C3%B6pr%C3%BC%27n%C3%BCn_Panoramik_Foto%C4%9Fraf%C4%B1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stone_Bridge_(Adana)","wikidata":"Q1438450"},{"title":"Stone Bridge (Saint Petersburg)","blocks":[{"text":"The Stone Bridge is a bridge across the Griboyedov Canal in Saint Petersburg, Russia. It was built in 1774–78, and at the time it was one of the first bridges in Saint Petersburg made of stone, hence the name. The bridge crosses the canal on the axis of Gorokhovaya Street connecting Kazansky and Spassky islands. Unlike many other bridges, this one did not undergo major reconstruction, and therefore preserves most of its original form from the 18th century."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Kamenny_Bridge_SPB_%28img1%29.jpg/330px-Kamenny_Bridge_SPB_%28img1%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stone_Bridge_(Saint_Petersburg)","wikidata":"Q1967331"},{"title":"Stone Bridge (Regensburg)","blocks":[{"text":"The Stone Bridge in Regensburg, Germany, is a 12th-century bridge across the Danube linking the Old Town with Stadtamhof. For more than 800 years, until the 1930s, it remained the city's only bridge across the river. It is a masterwork of medieval construction and an emblem of the city."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Regensburg_-_Steinerne_Bruecke_ohne_Dom.jpg/330px-Regensburg_-_Steinerne_Bruecke_ohne_Dom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stone_Bridge_(Regensburg)","wikidata":"Q695095"},{"title":"Rock (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a rock is any naturally occurring solid mass or aggregate of minerals or mineraloid matter. It is categorized by the minerals included, its chemical composition, and the way in which it is formed. Rocks form the Earth's outer solid layer, the crust, and most of its interior, except for the liquid outer core and pockets of magma in the asthenosphere. The study of rocks involves multiple subdisciplines of geology, including petrology and mineralogy. It may be limited to rocks found on Earth, or it may include planetary geology that studies the rocks of other celestial objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg/330px-Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_(geology)","wikidata":"Q8063"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/DAVIS%281879%29_p097_ADANA%2C_THE_KIZIL_DAGH_MOUNTAINS_IN_THE_DISTANCE.jpg/500px-DAVIS%281879%29_p097_ADANA%2C_THE_KIZIL_DAGH_MOUNTAINS_IN_THE_DISTANCE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/DAVIS%281879%29_p097_ADANA%2C_THE_KIZIL_DAGH_MOUNTAINS_IN_THE_DISTANCE.jpg/1280px-DAVIS%281879%29_p097_ADANA%2C_THE_KIZIL_DAGH_MOUNTAINS_IN_THE_DISTANCE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/DAVIS%281879%29_p097_ADANA%2C_THE_KIZIL_DAGH_MOUNTAINS_IN_THE_DISTANCE.jpg/500px-DAVIS%281879%29_p097_ADANA%2C_THE_KIZIL_DAGH_MOUNTAINS_IN_THE_DISTANCE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Taşköprü, with Adana and the Kizildag mountains, from a view by Edwin John Davis published in Life in Asiatic Turkey in 1879.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADAVIS(1879)_p097_ADANA%2C_THE_KIZIL_DAGH_MOUNTAINS_IN_THE_DISTANCE.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/DAVIS%281879%29_p229_ADANA%3B_THE_AJEMI_CAFE_AT_THE_BRIDGE.jpg/500px-DAVIS%281879%29_p229_ADANA%3B_THE_AJEMI_CAFE_AT_THE_BRIDGE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/DAVIS%281879%29_p229_ADANA%3B_THE_AJEMI_CAFE_AT_THE_BRIDGE.jpg/1280px-DAVIS%281879%29_p229_ADANA%3B_THE_AJEMI_CAFE_AT_THE_BRIDGE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=p